{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>The three cities</b> – And even though in the land of Canaan there were nine tribes, while here there were only two and a half, He equalized the number of their cities of refuge, because in Gilead murderers were numerous, as it is written: \"Gilead, a city of workers of iniquity, tracked with blood\" (Hosea 6:8). This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 35:14), from the words of the Sages (Makkot 9a). And even though refuge is only for those who kill unintentionally, there were those who shed blood with guile and presented themselves as unintentional killers, so it was necessary to multiply for them cities of refuge to receive all of them, so that the intentional killer would not be known. And if so, the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded this concerning the future, in the manner of \"For this people will rise up and go astray\" (Deuteronomy 31:16), or they may say that the air of the land of Gilead produced murderers from the time it was a nation.\n\nAnd I am astonished, for according to the opinion of the Sages (Makkot 10a), the verse that says \"and to them you shall give forty-two cities\" (Numbers 35:6) — He commanded all of them for refuge. Now, among them there were thirty-six cities in the land of Canaan and six in Transjordan, and all of them serve as cities of refuge according to the Sages. And so the cities of refuge were distributed throughout all the land of Israel fairly and equally, for four cities of refuge per tribe would result. He also counted the tribe of Manasseh in the land of Canaan because the greater part of it was there. And perhaps HaShem multiplied the designated cities in Transjordan in honor of Moses, that he would designate half of them, but in their totality they were in measure and in number. And perhaps in the cities designated for the Levites, HaShem increased them in Transjordan in honor of Moses so that he himself would designate half of them, but they were all included together in proportion and in number.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, it appears to me that the land of Transjordan was very large, for it comprised the territories of two great Amorite kings concerning whom the verses are expansive, even though Ammon and Moab were careful regarding them. And the kings of the land of Canaan were kings of towns — every ruler of a city was called a king, as you see: \"The king of Jerusalem, one; the king of Hebron, one\" (Joshua 12:10), and between them is a journey of half a day. And so the Sages mentioned (Genesis Rabbah 32:2) between Bethel and Ai a distance of four mil, yet one had a king and the other had a king. And it is possible that this was the custom in those generations, that every lord of a city was called a king, or it was in honor of the land of Israel, according to the midrash of the Sages. In any case, they were not kings of countries but only kings of city after city. And so it is written: \"Seventy kings, with their thumbs and their great toes cut off, gathered food under my table\" (Judges 1:7). And so Transjordan was very large, worthy of three cities of refuge just like all the land of Israel from the Jordan onward, and these six cities alone served as cities of refuge, while the forty-two were Levite pasturelands, not for refuge."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "In the second paragraph, Ramban first mentions the opinion of the Sages (Makkot 10a) that all 48 Levitical cities served as cities of refuge; the text contains a brief aside about HaShem multiplying cities in Transjordan in honor of Moses that appears to restate the same idea, possibly a duplicated gloss. Also, the phrase וְאַף כִּי עַמּוֹן וּמוֹאָב טָהֲרוּ (ס״א נזהרו) בָּהֶם presents a textual variant: 'purified themselves' vs. 'were careful concerning them,' both rendered in context as the nations having abstained from those territories."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:37.715832632Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}